# Line-ending policy for a repo shared between a Linux seat and a Windows laptop.
#
# Every tracked file is LF today (verified: 0 CR bytes across all blobs), so this file changes nothing
# retroactively. It exists so a Windows checkout cannot inject CRLF into a later commit: with
# `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows, git converts on checkout and back on commit, but that only protects a
# repository that already states its policy. Without this file, a tool that rewrites a file (rustfmt, an
# editor, a formatter with its own defaults) can stage CRLF and nothing in CI looks for it.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary or generated content must never be line-ending converted.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf binary

# Test fixtures that may intentionally contain CRLF (mail-style payloads) are left alone.
*.crlf.txt -text
